ountability to  corporations is not accountability to  governments it's not accountability to  us  the question is accountability to the  people who are going to be affected by  these  programs why not have a program an  approach  which says we are not we are going to  investigate the performance of every  program out there  but we're going to go to the people  we're going to ask them  is that this school working is this dam  actually  doing you any good is this privatization  of this utility allowing you to get  services  at affordable rates is the financial  sector review allowing you to get credit  or affordable rates and what you're  going to find is the answer those  questions is no  but i don't hear anybody out here saying  we should turn over accountability  to the people who are affected you know  i don't hear that i see it in the book  so i asked you is that something that  you would approve of that you would  that you would agree with and can we  work towards the mechanisms of  empowering people overseas not us here  in this room  but people overseas to determine what is  you know accountable programs  thank you um i agree with you completely  about accountability  but i think you that there are two  points from which i  want to disagree with you first it isn't  true that  the market system has failed the poorest  people in the world we've had enormous  progress in china  and india right not because of the bank  but because of the market system that  they've introduced the market system  they made the reforms  that's really like other countries to do  that is and the bank could help them to  do it but if  if they can do it by themselves as well  as with the bank and in many cases in  the case of china and  india they did do it largely by  themselves although india certainly we  see in  china certainly received world bank aid  it's hard to make a connection between  that aid and then the reforms so the  poorest people are  that's why poverty has dropped it's  dropped and that's part of the criticism  poverty has dropped  because of the effectiveness of opening  markets establishing the rule of law and  so on  and and not because so much because of  what the bank does second  i think you're missing the connection  between accountability  and things like the millennium challenge  account and output  based aid i mean the whole idea of  output-based aid is that we're going to  give assistance to those countries  that succeed in raising living standards  and doing things  that they promise to do and that gives a  great measure  of accountability and ownership within  the country the model that fails  is the model that the great god  wolfensohn sitting in washington  can order improvements in the lives of  people living in  goodness knows where but certainly  sub-saharan africa  allows many other poor countries i mean  that model has failed  what they can do is help countries that  want to do the things that will help  them to develop  this whole panel is an elaborate way of  taking the in your book sebastian of  taking the spotlight  off the evil corporations that really  manipulate this whole thing  i have a few sort of reactions to some  of the issues that have been raised and  i'll be really quick  first i think this debate between alan  and sebastian on who was there first on  selectivity is truly  silly i agree um good um  the fact is that alan blames um  wolfensohn for not  doing selectivity but let's look at what  the bank does  not what wolfenson seems to have said as  sebastian points out the bank does  use a system called the ida  performance based allocation which is  moving in that direction  and let us see how successful the  millennium  challenge corporation which hasn't spent  a penny yet by the way  will be with this great idea of output  based aid which we can all endorse we  can all endorse accounting  accountability to the people let's see  if it happens  it's going to turn out that it's bloody  complicated  i promise you so in two or three years  we will all be sitting here  worrying about why the millennium  challenge corporation  has repeated all the worries and all the  mistakes of  of the world bank and is not making much  progress that's my prediction i hope i'm  wrong  second i think sarat asked a really  important question  what is the objective function of the  world bank but let's keep in mind  that it is not the bank in some vague  way  that that addresses that question as  sebastian beautifully points out in his  book  it's the shareholders and for the most  part  it's the rich country shareholders it's  the us  and sometimes the u.s pushes the bank to  do things  that it might not otherwise do sometimes  the g7  as sebastian points out demands  everything from the bank  and then complains about lack of focus  if you  want to see some reforms that make sense  not radical changes but reforms that  make sense  at the world bank look to the right kind  of pressure on your own government  and keep in mind that for all the  complaining the united states  doesn't want to give up one bit of its  capital share in the bank  it's a big it's a great deal that the  rich countries have  here it's politically virtually costly  it's not costing in any politically  visible way  their taxpayers anything and in fact the  rich countries as a group are in a nice  happy  collusion with many of the middle-income  and poor countries  in which they admit that they want their  hands tied  they don't want to be pushed by  short-term political motives  to do things the way the bilateral  donors do them  that's why the world bank is is  something that we need  it's a healthy um  constraint in some ways on the the much  worse sins  of the bilateral donors and finally i  would say about  this issue of middle-income countries i  think it's ironic  that we have a plea from the right  which believes in choice and incentives  that they want to see china and brazil  and turkey  graduated when china and brazil and  turkey  want to be borrowers from the world bank  they have their reasons let's ask why  sometimes it's financial they need  counter cyclical financing when they're  in trouble and sometimes as in the case  of china  it's because they see the loans as a  very sensible vehicle  for buying good advice so  let's get back to where sebastian  started which is in the  in that awkward middle and think about  the reasonable changes and the  reasonable reforms  that could make a good institution work  even better  mr malabi what about the corporations  yeah um well i actually view mr  hedinger's  comments as sort of an example of what i  mean by when i say  uh that ngo critics don't always have an  off switch i mean  as he pointed out he was engaged with  the world bank uh in the structural  adjustment policy review initiative  for quite some time starting in 95 96  somewhere around then  um the bank you know invited advice  uh moved away not in the policy advice  terms because i think basically  structural adjustment was correct in  terms of  pressing market signals uh pressing the  idea that you shouldn't  sort of smother market signals but  structural adjustment was wrong  in the way that it was not politically  legitimate you couldn't force it down  people's throats  you needed to come to a development  consensus in a country if you wanted  those policies to stick so  that's why the bank moved more towards  sort of participatory  development where you would have a  national conference you'd bring in  churches trade unions  local ngos in the country and you would  have a  try and build a consensus in favor of  policies that would include sensible  macroeconomics but would also include  you know stuff for health stuff for  education and so on and so  there was a lot of shift uh under  wolfenstein a huge amount of shift  towards bringing ngos around the table  not only in dialogues  with mr hellinger but also in developing  countries  with the ngos locally and i tell stories  in my book about wolfenstein going out  to field offices and yelling at the  local staff because they didn't have  sufficiently good  contacts with uh with ngos in country  and forcing them to open their doors and  then there was huge numbers of outreach  officials  at the world bank whose job it is to  reach out to ngos in whichever country  you're talking about  but did anything change as a result well  yes i think they did for example in 1999  uh it was laid down that if you wanted  to get a soft loan a subsidized loan  from the world bank  you were supposed to have a development  plan created in this participatory  fashion  participating with ngos  so you know yeah there was a lot that  happened but mr hedinger is telling us  that nothing happened and therefore you  have to go and  criticize and demonstrate in the streets  i just don't agree i think this sort of  overly static view of the bank  and frankly also of poverty is the  problem poverty is on track  between 1990 and 2015 to halve  that millennium development goal is  going to be reached it's not static  that's just wrong and nor is the bank  static in 1985  the world bank had five  environmentalists a ridiculously low  number  now it's got a huge environmental  department and it's had it since  the early 90s so the bank changes in the  face of criticism but the critics don't  always change  their line that's the problem we have  time for one or two more  madame that  hello my name is kate saunders i'm from  london um  i wanted to pick up on uh sebastian's  point about the qinghai project  this is a critical chapter in the book  because china and the world bank  obviously have a very important relation  relationship um at the time in 99 i  broke the story as a journalist of this  project and reported on it for an  organization called tibet information  network  and i mentioned in the book i mentioned  as a campaigner at the time i wasn't a  campaigner  i was very concerned 