 them now but the  issue of prison labor that sebastian  mentions  as the accusation of possibly prison  labor being used as a low blow  they're made by the ngos in fact this  area was built up  by prison labor from the 1950s  i'm the author of one of the few books  in english on the chinese  lao guy system so i have a little bit of  background on that  sebastian also mentioned that the ngos  poured over policy documents to explore  where the bank had contravened its own  guidelines  in fact many senior members of bank  staff were also doing the same and  agreed with that  um including jan piercy who was the  executive director of the world bank in  the us  at the time one of the most serious  misunderstandings made in the book  though  is is about the beijing's policy on  western development which was initiated  by jansen  in 1999 and in my dialogue with the bank  at that time some very far sighted  individuals within the bank were  beginning to  get to grips with the significance of  that campaign in terms of its  far-reaching transformation of  tibetan areas and i think that the  the book does mispresent that in a way  that  senior bank staff at the time were  becoming very aware of that  just final point which is that uh the  the funding of the resettlement project  by the world bank would have set  a precedent um by an international  organization  funding beijing's strategic objectives  of demographic and economic  restructuring in the area  that point isn't conveyed clearly in the  book thank you  sebastian you whitewash the communist  policy to use slave labor and  cultural genocide my my first comment is  that  also attack of supporting insider  corporate  privileges my my first comment would be  that uh  my analogy about um critics of the bank  not always having an off switch i'm  beginning to like it more and more  um no look i mean i can't recite uh  you know 70 or 80 pages of footnotes i  have in the book  back to you but i don't think it's  inaccurate  