endent  evaluation  that's i think something that needs to  go forward and at the center  we have a working group that is trying  to figure out how to get  all of the donor community uh conspiring  and cooperating on some sort of the  mechanism because the problem in this  in this um area  justice sebastian says is that  unfortunately the incentives for donor  groups  and organizations like the world bank  are  to try to stay as quiet as possible  about those failures  because they open themselves up to all  of these attacks  especially from the right which  ultimately may undermine  our ability as a global community to  deal with this big challenge of global  poverty  so on that that's what i would say i  hope every person in this room  in addition to pushing the next world  bank president to read this book  will think about pushing the u.s  government to take some leadership  on this issue of independent evaluation  thanks  sebastian you get the last word well um  the bank uh  probably ought to do more on assessment  of its own performance but it does have  the operations evaluation department uh  internally has had it for uh 30 years or  something  uh maybe 25 and the head of this  operation told me a story which comes  from  june 1995 when he was parking his car in  the world bank garage and getting out  and he was almost knocked over by this  hectic and furious figure who was  sprinting towards him in the kappa  yelling at him  i've seen your report i want to shoot  myself uh  and this this crazy guy was jim wilson  who had just been appointed  president of the world bank had read a  report from the operations evaluation  department and was saying he was going  to shoot himself  because the performance of his own  institution was so appalling that he was  determined determined  to do better evaluation to kind of come  finato to change the performance to  shake it up  turn it upside down throw it out the  window catch it spin it around  do whatever it took uh to